  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing Critical Food Insecurity and Malnutrition of Returnees from Sudan in South Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Due to years of protracted armed conflict, increased poverty, economic collapse and pervasive environmental degradation, South Sudan is in the midst of a prolonged humanitarian crisis. Humanitarian needs continue to rise with an estimated 9.4 million South Sudanese in need of humanitarian aid and protection services in 2023. South Sudan remains one of the most food-insecure countries in the world with around 6.6 million people experiencing high levels of acute food insecurity. Moreover, the outbreak of fighting in Sudan on 15 April resulted in an influx of people fleeing the country, with more than 73,000 individuals crossing border entry points along the Sudan-South Sudan border. The conflict in Sudan has already affected South Sudan&apos;s economic outlook, leading to an increase in food prices and exacerbating food insecurity and negative coping mechanisms. 
 
The International Organization for Migration (IOM) aims to reduce food insecurity and malnutrition among vulnerable populations in Northern Bahr el Ghazal state, including returnees fleeing from the Sudan crisis, through providing adequate access to quality treatment for severely wasted children and promoting adequate maternal, infant and young child nutrition, combined with cash-based assistance.</narrative>
  </description>
